{"attack": true, "case": "The target's claim uses the word 'inevitably,' asserting that preference for corroborated theories necessarily smuggles in induction. Yet the target's own countercondition #3 — 'If acting on a theory does not involve any expectation of successful outcomes' — identifies a live escape route that the claim never closes off. The mechanism's load-bearing assertion that preferring a survived theory 'implies an expectation that it will continue to work' is a bare non sequitur. A methodological rule can rationally select the best-tested theory simply because all falsified alternatives have been eliminated and action is compulsory — a pragmatic, eliminative rationale that requires no inductive bridge from past survival to future success. Popper explicitly argued that corroboration is a past-tense report of severity of testing, not a predictor of future performance, and that acting on the best-tested theory is a policy choice among available options, not a belief in its probable truth. The target conflates pragmatic rationality (choosing the least-bad available option) with inductive inference (expecting future success), then declares the conflation 'inevitable' while simultaneously listing the disconfirming condition as a countercondition. A claim of inevitability that acknowledges a countercondition it fails to rule out is internally inconsistent.", "premise": "The claim asserts induction is 'inevitably' smuggled in, but enumerates a countercondition (#3: acting on a theory without expecting successful outcomes) that would break the inevitability, without providing any argument that the countercondition cannot be satisfied.", "premise_evidence": [{"block": "650344031ab2731f65", "quote": "The preference for corroborated theories inevitably smuggles induction back into Popper's system because any rationale for preferring survival over non-survival implicitly assumes the uniformity of nature."}, {"block": "650344031ab2731f65", "quote": "If 'acting on' a theory does not involve any expectation of successful outcomes."}], "counterexample": ["A scientist must choose between theory A (survived severe tests) and theory B (falsified). Acting on A over B requires no inductive expectation that A will succeed tomorrow; it requires only that B is eliminated and action is compulsory. This eliminative rationale satisfies countercondition #3, so induction is not 'inevitably' smuggled in."], "successor_question": "Does a methodological rule that selects the least-falsified available theory for compulsory action constitute an inductive inference, or is it a purely eliminative pragmatic choice that requires no expectation of future success?"}